%S A398787 60,84,90,126,132,140,150,156,198,204,220,228,234,260,276,294,306,308,
%T A398787 340,342,348,350,364,372,380,414,420,444,460,476,490,492,516,522,532,
%U A398787 550,558,564,572,580,620,630,636,644,650,660,666,708,726,732,738,740,748,774,780,804,812,820,836
%N A398787 Even numbers m such that A001221(m) >= 3 and A001222(m) = A001221(m) + 1.
%C A398787 These are the even terms of A398316. A398316 is the disjoint union of A398569 and this sequence.
%C A398787 Since all the terms are even, the prime 2 appears with an exponent of 1 or 2: either 2 is the only prime appearing with an exponent of 2, or the unique prime appearing with an exponent of 2 is an odd prime.
%C A398787 The asymptotic density of this sequence is (2/Pi^2) * (Sum_{p prime} 1/(p*(p+1)) + 1/3) = A185197 * (A179119 + 1/3) = 0.1344660297679805099613... . - _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 10 2026
%F A398787 Numbers of the form p^2*q_1*q_2*...*q_r, where r >= 2, p,q_1,q_2,...,q_r are distinct primes, and at least one of these primes is 2.
%F A398787 Equivalently, the sequence is the disjoint union of numbers of the forms:
%F A398787   1. 4*s, where s is odd and squarefree and A001221(s) >= 2, and
%F A398787   2. 2*p^2*s, where p is an odd prime, s is odd and squarefree, gcd(p,s) = 1, and A001221(s) >= 1.
%F A398787 Intersection of A398316 and A005843.
%F A398787 Equals (A072357 intersect A005843) \ A054753.
%e A398787 60 is a term because 60 = 2^2*3*5, so 2 is the unique prime occurring with exponent 2.
%e A398787 90 is a term because 90 = 2*3^2*5, so 3 is the unique prime occurring with exponent 2.
%e A398787 315 is in A085987 but not in this sequence, since 315 = 3^2*5*7 is odd.
%e A398787 420 is in this sequence but not in A085987, since 420 = 2^2*3*5*7 has four distinct prime factors.
%t A398787 nn = 840; s = Select[Range[2, nn, 2], And[PrimeNu[#] > 2, SquareFreeQ[#]] &]; Union@ Flatten@ Map[Function[{k, w}, Map[If[# < nn, #, Nothing] &[k*#] &, w]] @@ {#, FactorInteger[#][[;; , 1]]} &, s] (* _Michael De Vlieger_, Aug 11 2026 *)
%o A398787 (PARI) isok(k) = if(k == 1 || k % 2, 0, my(e = factor(k)[ ,2]); if(#e < 3, 0, e = vecsort(e, , 4); e[1] == 2 && e[2] == 1)); \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 10 2026
%o A398787 (PARI) isok(k) = my(f=factor(k), o=omega(f)); k%2 == 0 && o >= 3 && bigomega(f) == (o+1); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 12 2026
%Y A398787 Cf. A001221, A001222, A054753, A396919, A398569.
%Y A398787 Cf. A179119, A185197.
%Y A398787 Subsequence of A005843, A072357, A126706, A332785, A363101, A391026, and A398316.
%K A398787 nonn
%O A398787 1,1
